<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry the Lion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1129–1195</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Henry III, Duke of Saxony</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry the Lion was a Welf prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-of-saxony">Duke of Saxony</a> from 1142 and Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1156, the most powerful German noble of his age. He conquered Slavic lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, and set up bishoprics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratzeburg">Ratzeburg</a> and Schwerin. After he refused to aid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>, his duchies were stripped from him in 1180 and divided among rivals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion.jpg" alt="Henry the Lion" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_the_Lion_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
